#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548128} :activity/id #uuid "39c575ad-87f7-577c-b7de-503df313a6fa" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048204 :topic/id #uuid "881a9e15-1557-584f-9d93-ff8b0046998b" :topic/math-academy-id 1020} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "108be319-2cac-5831-9175-1aabb3c72448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547351}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547351} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1a4447f-3bfa-5239-8811-1f6dff980305" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4c0fb001-b215-5979-89f5-05a01ae52266" :question/math-academy-id "q-350641" :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2n}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2(1)}=\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the second term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2(2)}=\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{4}}{\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}}={(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{9}{4}(\\frac{1}{9})}{(1-\\frac{1}{9})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(\\frac{8}{9})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{9}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "120cee62-a2ba-56d3-bd5a-73197dd03830"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548128 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548022 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2185bcec-d33c-5b85-a90f-3baf6d8e3134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547385} {:answer/id #uuid "28fce1c0-fdae-52e2-b558-861fa662bf1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547391} {:answer/id #uuid "5abc1e52-5007-58cf-923d-9f9cdea5bf21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547518} {:answer/id #uuid "5e40558d-2c08-52ce-bd22-49714e884a17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547522} {:answer/id #uuid "69067c60-3e9e-5a7c-8919-986fa0b1106b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547550}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547550} :answer-field/id #uuid "da4c8753-aa62-5b12-846b-3fa944adeb01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "84522aa8-0251-51f3-8f96-c1848baee4b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-67300" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=10{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}=5.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{10{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}{10{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8661678d-4c4b-54f6-b95b-86b213e25dda" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548173}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548156 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d0264cc-d7dc-5c37-b30f-16424540ced2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547586} :answer-field/id #uuid "282a0a0f-efee-52cd-8a05-a07f215cbb39" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b85d1cfe-34fb-5d55-ad84-699fc2c94f6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-350640" :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2n}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2(1)}=\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the second term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2(2)}=\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{4}}{\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2}}={(-0.4)}^{2}=0.16.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2}}{1-0.16} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}(0.16)}{0.84} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.4}{0.84} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c0968cfd-c4e6-58c4-bbe3-c52236c84b10" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548086}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548173 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fcd25c1-b9a9-56eb-b404-446e379f595c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547378} {:answer/id #uuid "689eccb5-cccc-5071-a58b-3df40833a9ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547547} {:answer/id #uuid "8c692d74-d882-579c-a263-2ba81452e19d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547625} {:answer/id #uuid "a6af4e95-a3bd-547c-96b0-c9157f6909e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547683} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e40d2d-f0bc-584c-b6d9-b4b71e9295ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547745}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547378} :answer-field/id #uuid "56ebf041-55c8-5fe4-affb-3c90d8aaeea7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0515d331-a2ed-5378-ab7b-e5eca8ff6d75" :question/math-academy-id "q-78590" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $3.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{3},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =\\frac{4}{9}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{3}=\\frac{4}{9}(\\frac{27}{64})=\\frac{3}{16} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{4}{9}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{4}=\\frac{4}{9}(\\frac{81}{256})=\\frac{9}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}}=\\frac{(\\frac{9}{64})}{(\\frac{3}{16})}=\\frac{9}{64}\\cdot \\frac{16}{3}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{16})}{(1-\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{16})}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "da72f953-d706-5801-a724-042fb2b1439b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548177}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548177 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548019 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d5afcd8-4cf8-5778-a410-d996de8caa7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547343} {:answer/id #uuid "3b943b2d-ea0b-5325-b2aa-3e8653e1e477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547444} {:answer/id #uuid "6ef15147-7c05-56d2-a62e-10a55dfa362d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547562} {:answer/id #uuid "a306fe67-74bc-5773-80ce-c1513aa6afc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547673} {:answer/id #uuid "e32c4540-3e5c-5944-a86d-c76ecf3c6add" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547789}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547673} :answer-field/id #uuid "be9f989d-d56a-54a6-a664-496350494e54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7ac43aed-fdf1-5439-85aa-b688b91d01e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-124590" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{25}{27}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $3.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{3},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =\\frac{25}{27}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{25}{27}(\\frac{27}{125})=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{25}{27}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{4}=\\frac{25}{27}(\\frac{81}{625})=\\frac{3}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}}=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{25})}{(\\frac{1}{5})}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{25}{27}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{5})}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{5})}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e0ee7c2b-1160-544f-ad0f-fa7041d54fb9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548156}}] :activity/title "Sums of Infinite Geometric Series Given in Sigma Notation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547295}]}
